{
  // Type checking for everything the browser runs and everything the tools do.
  //
  // `allowJs` + `checkJs` + `noEmit` means TypeScript reads the source that actually
  // ships and reports on it. Nothing is compiled here — `tsconfig.kit.json` and
  // `tsconfig.tools.json` emit, and they set `noCheck`. Emit and check are separate
  // jobs with separate failure modes: a build that refused to emit until every
  // annotation existed would be a build nobody runs.
  //
  // **The kit is counted twice, and that is not a bug to fix.** The program holds
  // `assets/figures/src/kit/*.ts` because that is what an author edits, *and*
  // `assets/figures/kit/*.js` because `venn-regions.js` imports `./kit.js` and a
  // module resolves the file that ships. Excluding the generated copy does not remove
  // it — an import pulls it back in — so the count is roughly double for the kit and a
  // fix lowers it by two. The alternative, checking only the generated copy, would
  // check a stale artefact on the one change that matters.
  //
  // **Both of those sources are now at zero errors under `strict: true`, bar two files.**
  // The count came down in this order, measured with `tools types --list`:
  //
  //     checkJs, strict off                          0   (the ratchet's floor)
  //     strict on, before this pass                354
  //     after the runtime and the kit               55
  //     after the tools                             46
  //
  // and all 46 of those are in two commands — `commands/audit-figures.ts` (45) and
  // `commands/readout.ts` (1). Nothing else is left: every other number below is a work
  // list that has been worked.
  //
  // What the migration cost, so the next file is cheaper than this one was: the shape
  // behind about two thirds of the errors is a **destructured options argument**, and
  // the fix is to name the options once — a `@typedef` with `@property {T} [name]` lines,
  // referenced by `@param {Opts} [o]` — because a per-property `@param {object} o` with
  // `@param {T} o.name` on a destructured parameter is **ignored**, which is what made
  // these look unfixable. A required property makes the default `= {}` itself the error,
  // so a property that has a default is optional in the type, and one that is
  // dereferenced unconditionally is required and the default is written `= {} as Opts`.
  // In TypeScript the same interface does that job, plus a real annotation on the
  // parameter; JSDoc in a `.ts` file is documentation and nothing else.
  //
  // The ratchet stays: `tools types` fails when the count grows and when it falls
  // without the baseline being lowered, so it can only come down, and `strict` stays on.
  //
  // **`types: ["node"]` is not a convenience.** `tools/` is Node programs, and with
  // `types: []` TypeScript checked them as though Node did not exist: every one of
  // the eight `import fs from "node:fs"` lines reported TS2591, "cannot find name
  // 'node:fs', do you need @types/node", and `process` and `Buffer` were unknown
  // globals. Installing `@types/node` and naming it here took the count from 227 to
  // 190 in one change — 37 errors that were the checker's blindness rather than the
  // code's defect.
  //
  // The 212 figure modules under `books/*/docs/assets/figures/` are content and are
  // not in `include` yet. They call the kit, so checking them is the last step.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "strict": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "types": ["node"]
  },
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "assets/figures/kit.js", "assets/figures/kit/**"],
  "include": ["assets/**/*.js", "assets/**/*.d.ts", "assets/figures/src/**/*.ts", "tools/src/**/*.ts"]
}
